Simon Willisyn notes that he trained a tiny LLM years ago. He now runs good local LLMs on roughly

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:27.540
8GB of RAM. The observation highlights progress in local inference efficiency. Developers tracking

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.500
open source local tools can draw from his experience. The shift from early tiny models to current

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:40.800
capable ones on modest hardware shows rapid practical gains, data set auth github 1.0 according

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.660
to Simon Willison. The data set auth github plugin reached version 1.0. The release followed

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:53.100
fixes for cookie exploration issues. Those fixes support longer authenticated sessions.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:59.460
The update improves session handling for users of the plugin. Version 1.0 marks the resolution

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:05.540
of prior expiration limitations, experimenting with hyper surface constrained dynamic weight updating

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:11.000
according to the machine learning subreddit. A researcher shared results from a side project using

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.760
hyper surfaces to generate weight deltas. The deltas applied to a single decoder block iterated

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:23.880
multiple times. The approach reduces parameter count to about 16% of a standard 24-layer transformer.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.600
It maintains competitive loss throughout.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:32.640
The model uses a triangular wave function set and a context vector from gated linear attention

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.240
to modulate weight updates.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:40.100
Check out the hyper-surface weight update experiment when exploring parameter-efficient training methods.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.720
Let me pop the hood on token routing inside mixture of experts' layers,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.480
where the router itself adds a small feed-forward pass before the main computation.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:55.740
Routing decisions can be cashed across similar tokens to cut compute, yet accuracy falls

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.300
once the cash window passes a few hundred tokens.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.380
Quality gains from extra experts level off once the router underutilizes added capacity,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.740
typically near 64 experts for models under 30 billion parameters.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:15.200
An auxiliary load balancing loss makes expert distribution more uniform but can raise primary

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.180
task loss by up to 2%.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.720
The router is a lightweight two-layer network whose output directly decides which expert

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.060
weights load into memory.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.160
Caching router output for tokens within a small cosine distance of prior tokens, trims

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.740
router flops by roughly 30% on repetitive text, though the same cache degrades quickly

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.640
on code or multilingual inputs.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:43.180
Load balancing losses are scaled by a small coefficient, and raising that coefficient

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:46.940
past 0.01 often triggers the 2% regression.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.240
In production, the router decision occurs once

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:51.980
per token at the start of the layer

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.480
and stays reused for the rest of the block,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.440
keeping added latency to a few microseconds.

